            r1_brief: `9 a.m. From month 3 (March). The rule (Incoterms® 2020, ICC): CIP Veracruz —the winery contracts the carriage to Veracruz and insures it under the Institute Cargo Clauses (A), all risks, for at least 110% of the price; the risk passes to the importer when the pallets are handed to the first carrier at the winery—; FOB Bilbao, as every year —the risk passes when the goods are on board, but the container or the groupage pallets are handed to the terminal days before (the ICC keeps FOB and CIF for goods loaded on board, not containers)—; CIF Veracruz —the winery pays the freight and the minimum cover, clauses (C), but bears the risk until the goods are on board—; or DDP Mexico City, "all included": the winery pays and bears everything to the importer's door, clears the Mexican import through an agent (€400 a shipment) and carries the duties and charges it cannot recover there (10% of the value, stylised), and its shipments wait longer at customs (8 points less punctuality). The quote: a cost build-up of every leg the rule puts on the winery, the premium and a 5% contingency; only the ocean freight from the forwarder's rate sheet; last year's price, €2,806 whatever happens; or the cost of the legs plus 25% "just in case" —Mariana checks the freight line against her own forwarder's quote and takes part of her orders elsewhere (a padded freight cuts the volume by the same share as the pad)—.`,
