            r1_title: `Round 1 — Monday 11 January 2027: the Incoterm and the quote`,
            r2_brief: `9 a.m. From month 4 (April), when the winery contracts the main carriage (CIP, CIF, DDP; under FOB the importer's forwarder keeps shipping groupage from Bilbao). Tránsitos Malvarrosa S.L., your forwarder in Valencia, quotes: a 20-foot container with 10 pallets —truck to Valencia €950, clearance €140 a shipment, terminal €260, direct ocean freight to Veracruz €1,650, Veracruz terminal €420, import clearance €250 and the truck to the capital €1,100—; groupage a pallet —truck €85, clearance €140 a shipment, warehouse €40, ocean €160, deconsolidation €70, import €250 a shipment and a truck at €140 a pallet—; a 40-foot container with 20 pallets —€1,050, €140, €330, €2,400, €520, €250 and €1,350—. The load: a full 20-foot every 2 months; groupage every month, as every year; our own 20-foot every month, "safer", that sails half empty (5 pallets in a box for 10, and the load shifts); or a 40-foot every 4 months (the cheapest pallet, but four months of stock in the importer's warehouse). Each month between shipments beyond the first takes 5 points of punctuality (the importer runs short before the next box), and the stock costs it 25% a year. Expected losses in transit (breakage, wet, heat and theft): 1.0% of the value in a full 20-foot, 2.5% in groupage, 1.4% in a half-empty container and 1.2% in a 40-foot. The route: the weekly direct service Valencia–Veracruz of Naviera Golfo Atlántico (90% on time, the freight as quoted); transhipment at a Caribbean port (ocean ×0.85, 66% on time, losses ×1.3); Bilbao, the nearest port, as every year (truck ×0.45, ocean ×1.05 with a feeder, 78% on time, losses ×1.2); or whatever sails cheapest each booking (ocean ×0.90, 60% on time, losses ×1.25). Groupage loses 6 points of punctuality at the consolidator's cut-offs.`,
