            r2_title: `Round 2 — Monday 1 March 2027: groupage or container, and the route`,
            r3_brief: `9 a.m. From month 6 (June). Of the expected losses, 25% happen on the Spanish side (truck and terminal), 45% at sea and 30% in Mexico; each loss is paid by whoever bears the risk on that leg, unless an insurance covers it. The insurance: an annual open policy, clauses (A), warehouse to warehouse —from the winery to the importer's warehouse in Mexico City— for 110% of the value (premium 0.30%, 90% of each loss paid after the deductible); the cover the rule requires, as every year (CIP: clauses (A) from the winery to Veracruz; CIF: clauses (C) from on board to Veracruz; nothing under FOB or DDP); no insurance, "the carrier answers" (a carrier answers only up to the Hague-Visby limits, and under CIF or CIP not buying the cover breaches the sale: the winery pays the importer the cover it should have given); or a port-to-port cover bought shipment by shipment from the forwarder (clauses (A), premium 0.40% on half the journey, without the trucks; under CIP it leaves uncovered the leg from the winery to the port that the rule obliges the winery to insure). The documents: commercial invoice, packing list, transport document and the origin declaration of the EU–Mexico agreement checked before every sailing (€600 a year), and the importer pays no duty; as they come, as every year (no proof of origin: the general duty, stylised at 10% of the customs value; one shipment in six held for papers: 6 points of punctuality and €30 a pallet of storage); the origin sent afterwards, when the importer asks (half the entries pay the duty and wait for a refund; 3 points; €15 a pallet); or what Mariana asks: a second invoice at half the value "for the Mexican customs, like others do".`,
            r3_note: `Deliverable: which legs are insured and the documents each shipment leaves with. The cover must start where the risk starts.`,
            r3_title: `Round 3 — Monday 3 May 2027: the insurance and the documents`,
            r4_brief: `9 a.m. On Friday 1 October a fire broke out in the engine room of the Cebollera Star, which sailed on 28 September with the September shipment; it was put out with the help of a tug and the line has declared general average: every cargo owner contributes to the salvage and the sacrifices (25% of its cargo value, stylised) and part of the cargo suffers fire, smoke and water (15%); under Ley 14/2014 de Navegación Marítima, the line can hold the goods until each party gives a guarantee and signs the average bond. The cargo belongs to whoever bears the sea risk under the rule (the importer under FOB, CIF and CIP; the winery under DDP). What you do: ask the insurer for the general average guarantee and get the bond signed (released in 2 weeks; without insurance there is nobody to issue it and the winery ends up paying a deposit in 4 weeks); pay the cash deposit yourself so the cargo moves (4 weeks; the winery's money tied for 10 months at 6%); wait for the line and the adjuster, as every year (13 weeks: the importer runs out of wine and 80% of the sales of the weeks beyond the second are lost); or send the same pallets again by air at €2,100 a pallet so the importer never runs out. The same week the lines announce a 40% rise in ocean rates from January 2028. The 2028 plan: an annual contract with the direct-service line at the 2027 rate plus 5%, with priority loading (+3 points); keep buying spot and pay the rise, as every year; a cheaper deal with a consolidator (NVOCC) at 10% under the 2027 rate on transhipment services (−12 points); or Íñigo's idea: move the account to EXW from January "and let the importer worry about freight and risk" (the importer then books every leg, including the export clearance in Spain: €200 more a shipment and 5 points less).`,
